  "intro": "# King's Indian setups\n\nBlack plays ...Nf6, ...g6 and ...d6, fianchettos the dark-squared bishop and castles. The pawn goes to d6, not d5. That one difference changes what White should do.\n\n## What Black is doing\n\nThe bishop on g7 aims at d4 and the queenside. Black wants to break with ...e5, and if White closes the centre, to attack on the kingside with ...f5. Nothing is threatened early, so White has time to build.\n\n## Two changes to your setup\n\nThe bishop goes to e2, not d3. The pawn on g6 already defends h7, so d3 aims at nothing; on e2 the bishop supports the centre and stays out of the way.\n\nThe c-pawn goes to c4, not c3. Black declined to put a pawn on d5, so the centre is there for the taking. This is the one chapter where White gets a full big centre, and it is the reward for Black playing ...d6.\n\n## The h3 move earns its keep here\n\nPlay h3 early. When Black breaks with ...e5 and hits the bishop on f4, the answer is Bh2: same diagonal, no harm done, and the pawn on e5 becomes a target. Without h3 the bishop has to go to g3, where ...Nh5 can trade it off.\n\n## Typical mistakes at this level\n\nPutting the bishop on d3 out of habit, where the g6 pawn makes it useless. Playing c3 and settling for less than the position offers. Closing the centre with d5, which hands Black the standard ...f5 attack. Grabbing the pawn on e5 when it is defended twice.",
